.TH AUTOLOGIN 8 2026-11-04 autologin "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
autologin \- log a user in on tty1 without a password
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B autologin
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.RI [ config ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B autologin
logs the user named in
.I config
in on tty1 without asking for a password, and runs the script from
.I config
as that user with
.BR /bin/sh .
It is meant to be started by an init system service (OpenRC, systemd, runit or dinit) as root.
.PP
If
.I config
is not given,
.B /etc/autologin.conf
is used.
See
.BR autologin.conf (5)
for its format.
.PP
To start the session,
.B autologin
does the following:
.IP \(bu 2
creates
.BI /run/user/ uid
owned by the user with mode 0700;
.IP \(bu 2
makes tty1 its controlling terminal, gives it to the user with mode 0620 and switches to it;
.IP \(bu 2
drops to the user's uid, gid and supplementary groups;
.IP \(bu 2
changes to the user's home directory, or
.B /
if that fails;
.IP \(bu 2
clears the environment and sets
.BR HOME ,
.BR USER ,
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR SHELL ,
.BR PATH ,
.BR TERM ,
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
.B XDG_SEAT
and
.BR XDG_VTNR ;
.IP \(bu 2
runs the script as a login shell, so
.B /etc/profile
and
.B ~/.profile
are read first.
If the script is empty, an interactive shell is started instead.
.PP
.B autologin
stays in the foreground until the session ends.
Stopping it with
.BR SIGTERM ,
.B SIGINT
or
.B SIGHUP
passes the signal to the whole session.
When the session ends, the rest of its processes are killed and tty1 is given back to root.
.PP
With
.B relogin=no
in the config,
.B autologin
creates
.B /run/autologin.done
when the session ends.
When it is started again while that file exists, it runs a normal login prompt on tty1
.RB ( /sbin/getty ,
or
.B /sbin/agetty
if there is no getty) instead of logging the user in.
.B /run
is cleared on reboot, so the next boot logs in automatically again.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Check
.I config
without logging anyone in, and print what would run.
It reports an error if the user does not exist or the script has a syntax error
(checked with
.BR "sh \-n" ),
and warns if the user's home directory does not exist or the user is not in the
.B seat
group.
It does not need root.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/autologin.conf
Default config file.
.TP
.B /run/autologin.done
Created when a session ends with
.BR relogin=no .
.TP
.BI /run/user/ uid
Runtime directory created for the user.
.TP
.B /var/log/autologin.log
Where the service files send errors that happen before tty1 is taken.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Without
.BR \-\-check ,
the exit status of the session, or 1 if it could not be started or was killed by a signal.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-check ,
0 if the config is fine and 1 if it is not.
.SH NOTES
Nothing else may run on tty1.
Disable its getty first, for example by commenting out the tty1 line in
.B /etc/inittab
on busybox init.
The systemd service stops
.B getty@tty1
by itself.
.PP
Wayland compositors need
.BR seatd (1)
running, and the user has to be in the
.B seat
group.
.PP
The shell and the terminal are set at compile time with
.B SHELL
and
.B VT
in
.BR include/session.h .
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the installed config:
.PP
.RS
.EX
autologin \-\-check
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR autologin.conf (5),
.BR seatd (1),
.BR getty (8),
.BR agetty (8)
